				"4C050ED55B21CE58"
			]
			description: ["The Basic Steam Turbine is the first EU generation you can make. This machine takes the Steam you're already making and converts it into energy at a 2:1 ratio. This means that if you have, say, 640 mb of steam, it will be converted into 320 EU. Set the output side with a wrench."]
			id: "79EBCF054AB055AA"
			size: 1.5d
			subtitle: "Steamy!"
			tasks: [{
				id: "66C49713CFB813EB"
				item: "gtceu:lv_steam_turbine"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "Basic Steam Turbine"
			x: 2.5d
			y: -2.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["79EBCF054AB055AA"]
			description: [
				"This quest is important to read for players unfamiliar with GregTech energy and machine behavior. Those who have played before or don't need a refresher know what they're doing. It is split up into pages for your convenience."
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"&lEnergy Overview&r"
				"GregTech uses EU (standing for Energy Unit) rather than the RF or FE energy that most other mods use. This is because it operates in a way that is much more complicated than those, and this quest explains how it works along with a few other related things."
				""
				"EU is still fundamentally a unit of electrical power, like the aforementioned systems, but it differs in that it is also measured in Amps. One Amp can be treated as something like a \"packet\" of energy, and its size depends on the voltage tier. For example, one Amp of LV is 32 EU, and these packets are transferred or used up once per tick. These values correspond directly to how much energy a machine of the same tier can use. All LV machines can use, at most, 32 EU per tick (abberviated to EU/t), though the actual energy usage depends on the recipe being run in the machine."
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"&lCables&r"
				"Different cables, on top of being assigned a tier of energy it can transport, also transport different amounts of Amps, depending on the size. Both of these bits of information are found in the tooltip."
				"There is a distinction between &ocables&r and &owires&r. Cables are wires that are coated in rubber, which lowers the power loss (covered later) and also prevents you from taking damage by touching live wires, which can happen with bare wires. "
				"Superconductors are only available in wire form, though they do not have cable loss and do not damage you when live. These are also explained later."
				"You can interact with and mine cables using a Wirecutter, similar to using a wrench for most other GregTech blocks."
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"&lEnergy&r"
				"You can think of a cable having multiple Amps as a pipe that has multiple lanes. A cable that can transport 4 Amps of power can transport 4 instances of an LV Amp, or totalling to 128 EU/t, though again, the machines you'll be making cannot consume that entire figure."
				""
				"An important note is that all singleblock generators will generate exactly one Amp when consistently running. This means that, ideally, you should be matching the number of generators with the number of amps your cables can carry, if you want to be efficient. Multiblock generators are a bit more complex in this regard, but you will not be using these until EV and beyond, so it is not currently a concern."
				""
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"&lCable loss&r"
				"Just like in real life, cables lose energy over distance. The loss is noted in the tooltip, it is important to note this figure is loss &oper amp&r, not per total block. Tin Cables have loss of 1 EU, so for 1x cable, they simply lose 1 EU per block travelled. However, using 4x cables for an example, they actually lose 4 total EU per block, the single amp transported in each \"channel\" still lose 1 EU per block."
				"Superconductors are wires that are more complex to craft, but also have more amps than most (if not all) of the cables at their tier, do not have any cable loss, and do not damage you when live. They are also needed in some specific crafting recipes. Every tier has one superconductor that can be crafted."
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"&lThe danger zone&r"
				"You should not try to transport &4more&r amps than a cable can support. GregTech machines only \"ask\" for energy on demand, so you can also only consume as many amps as you have machines, but if you have, for example, 3 turbines and 3 machines connected together, and 2 of the machines are running recipes, connected together by 1x Tin cables, you will be attempting to transfer 2 amps of power over cables that can only support one. This will result in the cables &4vaporizing&r, which destroys the cable blocks. "
				"The rule of thumb here is to simply not connect more generators than a cable has amps. "
				""
				"You are freely able to connect lower tier energy to higher tier machines, such as connecting LV Turbines and Tin cables up to an MV machine. The machine will only try to pull one amp, so this is generally ill advised, but it can be useful if you know what you're doing. However, if you try to connect &ohigher&r voltage energy to a &olower&r voltage machine, this will cause the &4machine to overvolt and explode, voiding the machine and all items contained within.&r It is best to match voltages where possible, and be careful when playing with transformers."
				""
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"&lMachines&r"
				"As noted with the ULV covers, you can place any type of cover on machines to alter their behavior in some way. On top of this, you can also set an output slot, similar to how you set the vent port of Steam machines, and then choose to automatically output items or fluids through that side in the GUI. A benefit of CEu Modern is that you can actually desync these slots in the GUI and have items and fluids be output through different slots, no covers required. This was a feature in 1.12, but it required a late game item to configure."
				""
				"Present in the bottom left, you can set a different programmed circuit in crafting machines. Some recipes require this to be set, which is a feature present to avoid recipe conflicts. You can see the required circuit represented as an item in the crafting recipe in EMI. In prior versions of GregTech, this was actually an item that took up an input slot of the machine, but Modern has fully condensed programmed circuits into a GUI feature."
				""
				"You can use a Soft Mallet to manually turn any machine on or off, but for electric machines, you can also click the power button in the GUI for the same effect. "
			]
			id: "12B2289AE89B789E"
			subtitle: "Nerd emoji"
			tasks: [{
				id: "669D30E948FAFF57"
				title: "ok"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			title: "GregTech Energy/Machine Info Dump"
			x: 2.5d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["79EBCF054AB055AA"]
			description: ["These machines will phase out microcrafting in your crafting table. Most of them serve as replacements for some of the crafting tools you've been using up to this point."]
			id: "4B89ACC907ACC921"
			tasks: [{
				id: "64CA3AA792320CAD"
				title: "Convenient Machines"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			x: 6.0d
			y: -2.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B89ACC907ACC921"]
			description: [
				"The Wiremill mills wires. It creates wires at a 1:2 ratio of ingots to wires, which is vastly better than the 3:2 ratio you dealt with in the steam age. As you can imagine, there is no plate intermediate, it makes wires directly from ingots."
				"Most players choose to make this one first."
			]
			id: "470AB63333677769"
			tasks: [{
				id: "6A654428803C8132"
				item: "gtceu:lv_wiremill"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 5.0d
			y: -4.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B89ACC907ACC921"]
			description: ["The Bender finally allows you to create 1:1 plates from ingots, something you have been working towards since you first began working with metals."]
			id: "6293584FF2F0DC92"
			tasks: [{
				id: "0B21CACC0EB113AA"
				item: "gtceu:lv_bender"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 6.0d
			y: -4.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B89ACC907ACC921"]
			description: [
				"The Lathe is your rod creating machine, allowing for 1:1 rod creation with a small dust byproduct."
				"Most other modpacks opt for the \"easy\" version of this machine, which directly makes 2 rods out of 1 ingot. The \"hard\" version used here does actually yield this same amount, but you must turn the small dust back into ingots to run the recipe again. In MV, you gain access to the Extruder, which will make 2 rods directly (along with many other convenient recipes)."
				"You can likely imagine that this can be annoying to manage, so you have two options you can take:"
				"1. Continue to manually make rods with files, stomaching the worse material effieciency to opt for speed"
				"2. Create an automated setup to take ingots, create rods in a lathe, and deal with the byproducts in some way. There is a machine that can pack the dusts into full size dusts, and a machine that can smelt the dust into an ingot, which can then be piped back into the lathe again. "
				""
				"GregTech offers a solution to this problem, consider trying to solve it for maximum effieciency, a dopamine hit, and brownie points. Or, continue doing the manual process until MV when it stops being a choice."
			]
			id: "2D3CD351ECC5D198"
			tasks: [{
				id: "1B5C3041334E3BA4"
				item: "gtceu:lv_lathe"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 7.0d
			y: -4.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B89ACC907ACC921"]
			description: [
				"The polarizer will be predominantly used throughout the game to magnetize rods, replacing the need for the redstone recipe in a crafting grid you needed to use to get to this point. Upon entering MV, those motors require magnetic steel rods, which can onlly be made in this machine, but you can opt to continue to spend redstone on magnetic iron rods until then."
				"It's worth noting this machine is decently energy efficient and dirt cheap to craft, only requiring some wire, a couple rods, and a machine hull. No circuits or other components necessary!"
			]
			id: "67EE4DA36D47F6B9"
			tasks: [{
				id: "5E4BAB9B049D7609"
				item: "gtceu:lv_polarizer"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 8.0d
			y: -4.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B89ACC907ACC921"]
			description: [
				"The Assembler is perhaps the most important machine in the mod, given how many important recipes require it and how much it unlocks when you create it at every tier. It is placed in this section of the questbook, however, because it is most convenient right now for having massively more efficient and convenient circuit component recipes. Combine these with the other machines (particularly the wiremill), and circuits become more expensive in time to craft rather than in resource requirement."
				"To get the most out of the Assembler in this regard, you will also want a Basic Centrifuge, Basic Extractor, and Basic Arc Furnace for some recipes requiring fluids or more advanced materials."
			]
			id: "4D82CECF0A3730C3"
			tasks: [{
				id: "241C1802F871810D"
				item: "gtceu:lv_assembler"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 8.0d
			y: -2.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B89ACC907ACC921"]
			description: [
				"Similar to the Assembler, the Chemical Reactor is massively important for later progression, but it will be important currently as it can massively offset the cost of Rubber production. Currently, you need to combine 1 Sulfur and 1 Sticky Resin turned into Rubber Pulp in an Alloy Smelter to get one Rubber Ingot, and two of those are required for a rubber sheet, which allows you to coat a single 1x wire."
				"The Chemical Reactor, combined with the Fluid Solidifer, detailed in the next quest, can turn this recipe into 1 Sulfur, 3 Sticky Resin, which can make 9 ingots worth of Rubber, that can then be turned into plates at a 1:1 ratio. You're getting 9 times the value out of your sulfur, 18 when considering the sheets, while getting 3x/6x the value out of your rubber trees. It should be obvious that you want to get this up as soon as possible, and probably make a dedicated setup for it."
			]
			id: "5D04164CCEE0759F"
			tasks: [{
				id: "1077641CE23DAFEF"
				item: "gtceu:lv_chemical_reactor"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 8.0d
			y: -1.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B89ACC907ACC921"]
			description: ["The Fluid Solidifier can be used to make some things, such as Tin Rotors, more efficiently, but as detailed in the Chemical Reactor quest, you will want it for Rubber production currently."]
			id: "3CCAC30DA8A06C1E"
			tasks: [{
				id: "53A765904052AA3E"
				item: "gtceu:lv_fluid_solidifier"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 8.0d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B89ACC907ACC921"]
			description: ["As noted in the Assembler quest, you will want this for circuit creation. You can make Glue by centrifuging Sticky Resin in this machine, which is used in Resistors and Circuit Boards."]
			id: "7741579C77FA8DB0"
			tasks: [{
				id: "276BC865AC418376"
				item: "gtceu:lv_centrifuge"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 7.0d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B89ACC907ACC921"]
			description: ["A machine you should be familiar with, the Extractor in LV gains a fluid output slot. This machine can be used to liquify metals, among other things, which can be used in some recipes to maximize efficiency. Right now, you'll want to create some Liquid Red Alloy for more efficient Vacuum Tubes."]
			id: "69CB7AAA33F19A37"
			tasks: [{
				id: "4B7F6AFD1298C3CF"
				item: "gtceu:lv_extractor"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 6.0d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B89ACC907ACC921"]
			description: [
				"The Arc Furnace is distinct from a regular Furnace in that it requires Oxygen to run. It is used at this point to create Annealed Copper, an alternate version of copper used in some specific use cases, and also offers a 1:2 sand to glass recipe."
				"Yes, it does require Oxygen, something you cannot make yet. You will have plenty of it in due time."
			]
			id: "76734A43AEC9D1F2"
			tasks: [{
				id: "41E66841CE788A87"
				item: "gtceu:lv_arc_furnace"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 5.0d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B89ACC907ACC921"]
			description: [
				"The Cutting Machine acts as a saw, this can be used for more efficient bolt recipes. Combine with the Lathe for screws worth pocket change."
				"It requires Water, Distilled Water, or Lubricant for all recipes Distilled Water is more efficient in cost and time spent than regular Water, and Lubricant is even stronger."
			]
			id: "7F2BEB36501CABCB"
			tasks: [{
				id: "08E2563AD70890EE"
				item: "gtceu:lv_cutter"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 8.0d
			y: -3.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["79EBCF054AB055AA"]
			description: [
				"GregTech ore processing goes much more indepth than simply doubling ores in a slightly convoluted way. It involves a deep tree of recipes that process ores in many specific ways to not only get the most yield out of a single ore, but more importantly, produce byproducts different than that of the ore itself."
				"You do NOT need to, and really shouldnt, craft all or even most of these machines right now. Most of the processes on offer only become specifically useful later in the game, this portion of quests is only here to inform you that this exists. Unless you REALLY like the idea of some extra yield, any ore processing setup you've made up to this point will be serviceable, though a couple of these will be needed soon for some particular recipes."
			]
			id: "02F6E1C558B0CAA4"
			tasks: [{
				id: "1DDA7E88267227BD"
				title: "Ore Processing, Again"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			x: -0.5d
			y: -2.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["02F6E1C558B0CAA4"]
			description: ["You can choose to create a singleblock Macerator for your own convenience. As noted in the recipes in EMI, the chanced byproducts of recipes in this machine can only be produced in HV, MV or prior versions only produce the guaranteed outputs. &lThis is the only machine in the entire mod that behaves this way&r, all other machines produced chanced outputs at the noted rates (or above, if overclocked). For the purposes of ore processing, creating electric macerators before HV is actively detrimental, as the multiblock Steam Grinder you can already make is vastly quicker, cheaper, and more energy efficient. It can still be helpful to make one of these to augment your group of machines used for crafting as to not interrupt any processing you may be doing in your multiblocks."]
			id: "0C6CF5B71FCB7FFD"
			tasks: [{
				id: "2504ABF9B6848441"
				item: "gtceu:lv_macerator"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 0.0d
			y: -3.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["02F6E1C558B0CAA4"]
			description: ["The Thermal Centrifuge can take crushed dust and turns it into Refined Dust, yielding some byproducts in the process."]
			id: "75BA1F3E35E1955C"
			tasks: [{
				id: "5FDF1DC4A57E8A76"
				item: "gtceu:lv_thermal_centrifuge"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: -1.0d
			y: -3.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["02F6E1C558B0CAA4"]
			description: ["The Electromagnetic Separator separates refined dust, electromagnetically. It only has a few recipes."]
			id: "7819EA42B95A5EDD"
			tasks: [{
				id: "020444A1816DC4DE"
				item: "gtceu:lv_electromagnetic_separator"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: -2.0d
			y: -3.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["02F6E1C558B0CAA4"]
			description: ["The Chemical Bath can be described as a more specialized ore washer, needing things like Sodium Persulfate rather than water for some ore processing recipes. It also has some miscellaneous recipes like more efficient treated wood and oxidized copper recipes if those are your thing."]
			id: "5AE416B4DAB2EBAE"
			tasks: [{
				id: "28868290B2DEBDC1"
				item: "gtceu:lv_chemical_bath"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: -2.0d
			y: -1.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["02F6E1C558B0CAA4"]
			description: ["The Ore Washer, fundamentally, is an electric version of the cauldron washing process you may be familiar with. It requires water and washes impure dusts."]
			id: "4DCBFDAF0FBD5B73"
			tasks: [{
				id: "6620C49F2BB662CB"
				item: "gtceu:lv_ore_washer"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: -2.0d
			y: -2.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["02F6E1C558B0CAA4"]
			description: ["The Sifter is the premier gem ore processor. This machine allows you to generate greader yield for ores such as Diamond or Emerald, as well as some important GregTech gems. It takes Purified Ore and has many chanced outputs."]
			id: "30E552DAA16CBBEE"
			tasks: [{
				id: "19A2F25C83005CD6"
				item: "gtceu:lv_sifter"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: -2.0d
			y: -0.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["02F6E1C558B0CAA4"]
			description: ["The Autoclave is a machine that can turn gem dusts into their gem form, taking some fluid input and possibly not always producing the gem depending on the fluid used. Used with the Sifter for the best effect."]
			id: "03B02C9EAFC93A94"
