		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4798AEB718C0948B"]
			description: [
				"Yes, you do need two."
				""
				"If you've done any perusing of any EBF recipes, you may have noticed that there are next to no LV recipes present. To get one up and running, you need MV power. But, how can you do this with LV generators and infrastructure?"
				""
				"A quirk of EU as a system is that you can create higher tier voltage amps out of lower ones. While LV is 32 EU/t, MV is 128 EU/t, a 4x increase. Every voltage is 4x the energy of the last. You can use this fact to your advantage in two ways. First, you can create transformer machines that can take a voltage as input and output another one. These transfer the same &oamount&r of energy, but as a different voltage tier. "
				"What you need to do for the EBF, as you can only create LV energy hatches, is make two. Each energy hatch can take 2 amps of input. This does not mean anything when you have one energy hatch, but when you have two hatches, the amperage totals to 4. The game will buff up the energy tier of the multiblock to the next tier when doing this. This means that, for multiblocks, you can power them at a tier above what you're normally producing, assuming you make enough to do this. "
				""
				"Basically, produce 4 amps of LV, wire the turbines into 2 energy hatches that are part of one multiblock, profit."
			]
			id: "3F3A136A5AD3968C"
			tasks: [{
				count: 2L
				id: "7C5699C177E31A46"
				item: "gtceu:lv_energy_input_hatch"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 4.0d
			y: 6.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4798AEB718C0948B"]
			description: [
				"Maintenance is a mechanic new to Electric Multiblocks. Every multiblock requires its own Maintenance Hatch which should not be wallshared, and upon constructing a new multiblock, you must perform the maintenance operations with the tools specified in icon at the top left of the block GUI. This can be done by having those tools in your inventory and clicking on the dark icon in the center. "
				"Periodically, a multiblock will develop a maintenance problem that you need to manually fix. These can cause issues such as longer recipe times, and if too many develop, the machine will stop running recipes entirely. This, thankfully, happens &overy&r rarely, you are not even likely to run into any maintenance issues until HV and beyond, and by the time it would become annoying to manage, there are automated solutions to maintenance issues."
			]
			id: "1A36723A72447815"
			tasks: [{
				id: "1862D060E86D47C3"
				item: "gtceu:maintenance_hatch"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 5.0d
			y: 6.5d
		}
	]
	title: "&7Low Voltage"
}
